          "content": {
            "id": "34864b8d-840c-3a30-8974-0b794ff3cccc",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Carmakers accused of cheating emissions tests in UK lawsuits",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: Lawyers accused some of the world's biggest carmakers of manipulating diesel emissions tests.</p><p>The claims were made at London's High Court Monday by the legal representatives of 1.6 million claimants at the start of a huge trial.</p><p>Owners of diesel vehicles made by certain brands between 2012 and 2017 allege the companies cheated emissions tests and are suing.</p><p>The accused include Mercedes-Benz, Ford, Nissan, Renault, and Stellantis-owned Peugeot\u00a0and Citroen.</p><p>The manufacturers are accused of using unlawful \"defeat devices\" which detected when vehicles were being tested.</p><p>The devices allegedly ensured nitrogen oxide emissions were kept within legal limits under test conditions.</p><p>The claimants' lawyers argue the emissions were far higher when the cars were on the road, and in some cases up to 12 times test limits.</p><p>They said this caused harm to the environment and entitled consumers to compensation.</p><p>But the carmakers have called the claims fundamentally flawed.</p><p>They argue there are justified reasons why systems to control emissions are calibrated to work differently in certain conditions.</p><p>They also reject any similarity with 2015's 'Dieselgate' scandal, when Volkswagen admitted using defeat devices.</p><p>It cost the German auto giant billions of dollars.</p><p>The trial is due to conclude next year.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: Lawyers accused some of the world's biggest carmakers of manipulating diesel emissions tests.</p><p>The claims were made at London's High Court Monday by the legal representatives of 1.6 million claimants at the start of a huge trial.</p><p>Owners of diesel vehicles made by certain brands between 2012 and 2017 allege the companies cheated emissions tests and are suing.</p><p>The accused include Mercedes-Benz, Ford, Nissan, Renault, and Stellantis-owned Peugeot&nbsp;and Citroen.</p><p>The manufacturers are accused of using unlawful \"defeat devices\" which detected when vehicles were being tested.</p><p>The devices allegedly ensured nitrogen oxide emissions were kept within legal limits under test conditions.</p><p>The claimants' lawyers argue the emissions were far higher when the cars were on the road, and in some cases up to 12 times test limits.</p><p>They said this caused harm to the environment and entitled consumers to compensation.</p><p>But the carmakers have called the claims fundamentally flawed.</p><p>They argue there are justified reasons why systems to control emissions are calibrated to work differently in certain conditions.</p><p>They also reject any similarity with 2015's 'Dieselgate' scandal, when Volkswagen admitted using defeat devices.</p><p>It cost the German auto giant billions of dollars.</p><p>The trial is due to conclude next year.</p></body>",
